From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 19:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nss_freq: count idle samples in the CPU utilization average

cpu_load_ubi reports "Min 100%  Avg 0%  Max 0%" on an idle or lightly
loaded core, on every board that has been checked (IPQ5018 GL-B3000,
IPQ5018 LS3434, MX6200), and those are exactly the values
nss_freq_init_cpu_usage() writes: min starts at NSS_FREQ_CPU_USAGE_MAX,
max and used start at zero. Nothing had updated them.

nss_freq_compute_cpu_usage() rejects a sample when the usage it derives
is zero:

	actual_usage = (inst_cnt * 100) / max_ins;
	if ((actual_usage > NSS_FREQ_CPU_USAGE_MAX) || (actual_usage == 0))
		return;

max_ins is the instruction budget for a full millisecond (frequency /
1000, so 1e6 at 1 GHz), so a core running under 1% of that truncates to
zero and every one of its samples is thrown away. The early return also
skips the two lines at the bottom of the function that feed the
averaging window, so avg_ctr never reaches NSS_FREQ_USG_AVG_FREQUENCY
and the block that computes used, min and max never runs at all. The
counter is what turns the window into the advertised "averaged over 1
second"; with idle samples dropped it measures 1000 busy samples
instead, over an unbounded stretch of wall time.

Zero is a legitimate reading, not bad data. Keep the upper-bound
rejection, drop the zero test, and guard the division instead: max_ins
comes from the frequency table and is zero before the first frequency
is set.

While here, widen actual_usage and usage from uint8_t. usage is scaled
by 1/NSS_FREQ_CPU_USAGE_MAX_BOUND, so a fully busy core already
produces 133, and a firmware-supplied inst_cnt beyond ~2.5x the budget
wraps actual_usage modulo 256 - which can land back under 100 and pass
the sanity check with a meaningless value.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
---
--- a/nss_freq.c
+++ b/nss_freq.c
@@ -132,8 +132,8 @@
 static void nss_freq_compute_cpu_usage(struct nss_ctx_instance *nss_ctx, uint32_t inst_cnt)
 {
 	uint32_t estimated_ins_capacity;
-	uint8_t actual_usage;
-	uint8_t usage;
+	uint32_t actual_usage;
+	uint32_t usage;
 
 	spin_lock_bh(&nss_freq_cpu_usage_lock);
 
@@ -141,10 +141,21 @@
 	 * If actual CPU usage turns up higher than 100, there is something wrong with the received data.
 	 * Upper bound average varies between 80% usage to 100% usage.
 	 *
+	 * An idle core is not bad data: max_ins is the instruction budget for a
+	 * whole millisecond, so anything under 1% of it truncates to zero here.
+	 * Rejecting that sample used to skip the averaging counter as well, so on
+	 * a lightly loaded core the block below never ran and the file kept
+	 * reporting its initial values - min 100%, avg 0%, max 0%.
+	 *
 	 * TODO: To improve estimation algorithm for calculating how many actual instructions are executed.
 	 */
+	if (unlikely(nss_freq_cpu_status.max_ins == 0)) {
+		spin_unlock_bh(&nss_freq_cpu_usage_lock);
+		return;
+	}
+
 	actual_usage = (inst_cnt * 100) / nss_freq_cpu_status.max_ins;
-	if ((actual_usage > NSS_FREQ_CPU_USAGE_MAX) || (actual_usage == 0)) {
+	if (actual_usage > NSS_FREQ_CPU_USAGE_MAX) {
 		spin_unlock_bh(&nss_freq_cpu_usage_lock);
 		return;
 	}
